  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ニセアカシア」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%8B%E3%82%BB%E3%82%A2%E3%82%AB%E3%82%B7%E3%82%A2",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 110528753,
      "text": "ニセアカシア は北米原産のマメ科ハリエンジュ属の落葉高木。植物学上の標準和名はハリエンジュ（針槐）で、単に「アカシア」と称されることがあるが（後述）、アカシア属（Acacia属）のアカシアとは別の種である。日本には1873年に渡来した。蜜源植物として重要で、街路樹、公園樹、砂防・土止め用の植栽、材は器具用等に用いられる。季語は夏である。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Robinia pseudoacacia」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robinia_pseudoacacia",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1377028869,
      "text": "Robinia pseudoacacia, commonly known as black locust, is a medium-sized hardwood deciduous tree, belonging to the tribe Robinieae of the legume family. Another common name is false acacia, a literal translation of the specific name."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "toxicity": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Robinia pseudoacacia」の節「Toxicity」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robinia_pseudoacacia#Toxicity",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1377028869,
        "heading": "Toxicity",
        "text": "The bark, leaves, and wood are toxic to both humans and livestock. Important constituents of the plant are the toxalbumin robin, which loses its toxicity when heated, and robinin, a nontoxic glucoside.\nHorses that consume the plant show signs of anorexia, depression, incontinence, colic, weakness, and cardiac arrhythmia. Symptoms usually occur about 1 hour following consumption, and immediate veterinary attention is required.\nThe sawdust and shavings from Robinia lumber can cause contact dermatitis in sensitive persons.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
